{
  "summary": "This PR raises the minimum C++ standard for Bitcoin Core from C++20 to C++23 in CMake, depends, and CI environments, requiring GCC 13 or Clang 19. It removes internal polyfills and workarounds—migrating util::Expected to std::expected, adopting std::byteswap, and removing the ALWAYS_FALSE type trait—and applies C++23 features such as std::string::contains and std::views::zip across the codebase.",
  "problem": "Targeting C++20 requires maintaining custom polyfills, template workarounds, and older idioms in tree, even as related external dependencies like libmultiprocess already use C++23 features.",
  "evidence": [
    "Author notes in description: 'the workarounds to avoid C++23 keep accumulating and some parts of the code already use C++23 features, such as lambda attributes'",
    "Author notes in description that switching compiler standards unlocks future work tracked in #34979"
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [],
    "enables": [
      "#34979"
    ]
  },
  "scope_notes": "This is an RFC/draft PR. In addition to build system changes bumping compiler requirements to GCC 13+ and Clang 19+, it includes mechanical replacements of .find() with .contains() across over 50 files and replaces util::Expected with std::expected.",
  "changed_since_previous": "Minor adjustments to pass Include-What-You-Use (IWYU) checks and adjustments to unit tests for std::expected compatibility.",
  "card": "This pull request bumps the project's minimum C++ standard from C++20 to C++23, raising compiler requirements to GCC 13 and Clang 19 across CMake, depends, and CI. It replaces internal polyfills with standard library equivalents (std::expected and std::byteswap), removes template workarounds like ALWAYS_FALSE, and modernizes string searches with std::string::contains across 56 files. The author argues that workarounds to avoid C++23 continue to accumulate while related libraries like libmultiprocess already use C++23 features. This PR is an RFC draft and unblocks work tracked in #34979.",
